#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274876 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274876 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186273322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274876 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8efc2f1e-ef46-5622-8f4e-9dd18bfc0e59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186274228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274975 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274975 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274975 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186273933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274975 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c2759a57-ee5d-532e-a24f-4499af67035f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275200 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275200 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275200 #edb/ref 1071 "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ gets $36%$ of the copies.\n\n- Distributor $2$ gets $23%$ of the copies, and $92%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ gets $41%$ of the copies, and $95%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $3%$ of all copies have some printing defect, $70%$ of defective copies are sold, and $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cbae7601-74f3-53d4-af26-b2c79a262289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275200 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is given to distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.36 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.23 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.92 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.41 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.36+0.23+0.41=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold, which is represented as $P(S|{D}_{1}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.03)}{P(S|{D}_{1})(0.36)+(0.92)(0.23)+(0.95)(0.41)} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{D}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.025(0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011) & =0.021 \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.025} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011 \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.36}(\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011) \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & ≈0.664\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275221 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275221 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275221 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275221 #edb/ref 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(4,1).$\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | |  | | $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275221 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f2746c8-465f-5cd6-8d38-103cc9792ba5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275221 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,1) & ={f}_{X}(4)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278836 #edb/ref 1080 "A parallelepiped" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278836 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278836 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1acc367e-71f3-5e97-87ce-92842c5b3257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279124 #edb/ref 1080 "A pentagon" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279124 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279124 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3c423a78-0db9-5f90-983b-9dfbb047e9f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279153 #edb/ref 1080 "A parallelogram" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279153 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279153 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f4b22b4-65fa-5db1-94f1-780f0591f6e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279170 #edb/ref 1080 "A tetrahedron" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279170 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175